"If you had never been told, would you guess that a caterpillar and a butterfly are the same animal? One crawls and chews leaves. The other flies and sips from flowers. They have different mouths, different legs, and different eyes. And yet every butterfly was once a caterpillar. The story starts with an egg. A female butterfly lays it on a plant that her young can eat. Out of the egg comes a tiny caterpillar, and it starts eating at once. A caterpillar's whole life is eating and growing. Its skin does not grow with it, so from time to time the old skin splits and the caterpillar crawls out in a new, larger one. A monarch caterpillar does this in five steps. When it is fully grown, the caterpillar stops eating and sheds its skin one last time. What appears is a chrysalis, a hard case that hangs still. Inside, most of the caterpillar's body is broken down. The adult's body is built from small groups of cells that the caterpillar has carried since it hatched. These cells become the wings, the legs, and the eyes of the butterfly. At last the case splits, and the butterfly climbs out. Its wings are soft and folded. It hangs and waits while they open and dry, and then it flies away. Why go to so much trouble? One reason is that the young and the adults do not get in each other's way. The caterpillar eats leaves, and the butterfly drinks nectar, so they are not competing for food. Each body is built for one job.": "ad5174b99b26",
